  "notes": [
   "The breadboard image labels the lower half rows a-e and the upper half f-j, which is the reverse of the catalogue's naming (top half = rows a-e, bank 't'; bottom half = rows f-j, bank 'b'). I used catalogue naming: the wires go in the half nearest the Arduino's bottom edge, which I mapped to 't' rows a-e. The button straddles the centre gap, with its legs in the 't' and 'b' halves.",
   "The button's top legs are in column 10 and column 12 on the image's upper half. The lower legs are in the same columns on the lower half, where the wires connect.",
   "The catalogue's pushbutton joins 1.l with 1.r and 2.l with 2.r. The button spans the centre gap, so side 1 is on the 't' half and side 2 on the 'b' half. I put the two wires on the 't' strips, 10t and 12t. This is the same electrical result as the picture: pin 2 on one leg and GND on the other.",
   "The two wires are on different columns, 10 and 12. Pin 2 goes to column 10 and GND goes to column 12. Pressing the button joins those columns.",
   "The GND wire is drawn at the Arduino power header's second GND pin, which I named GND.2.",
   "Pin 2 uses the internal pull-up (INPUT_PULLUP), so there is no external resistor. Pin 13's onboard LED is built into the Uno and not modelled as a separate part.",
   "The Uno connects to the computer over USB for serial. The manual lists no USB cable part, so none is included."
  ]
